{"id":"waves-stories","name":"Waves & sound: stories","about":"Longer lessons that walk through one idea with several simulations","collection":"Lessons · Waves & sound: stories","presets":[{"name":"Lesson · What Is a Wave?","mode":"circle","transport":{"duration":33,"bpm":100},"visual":{"aspect":"9:16"},"overlay":{"layout":4,"name":{"enabled":false}},"audio":{"scale":"minorPentatonic","root":9,"noteLow":50,"noteHigh":86,"reverb":{"decay":3.5,"mix":0.35},"delay":{"time":0.375,"feedback":0.3,"mix":0.16}},"circle":{"rule":"travel","lab":{"travel":{"lambdas":"0.9","speed":0.45,"show":"rope"}},"look":{"trail":"lines","palette":"ocean","ambience":true},"sound":{"instrument":"synth-pluck","pitch":"angle","volume":0.6,"maxRate":22},"text":{"formula":"The beads only bob up and down…"},"combine":"sequence","layers":[{"id":"C2","enabled":true,"withPrev":false,"cfg":{"rule":"travel","lab":{"travel":{"lambdas":"0.9 0.5 0.3","every":6,"show":"both"}},"look":{"trail":"lines","palette":"ocean"},"sound":{"instrument":"synth-pluck","pitch":"angle","volume":0.6,"maxRate":22},"text":{"formula":"…a shorter wave is a higher note…"}}},{"id":"C3","enabled":true,"withPrev":false,"cfg":{"rule":"string","lab":{"string":{"modes":"1 2 3 4","every":4}},"look":{"trail":"lines","palette":"aurora"},"sound":{"instrument":"harp","pitch":"angle","volume":0.7,"maxRate":22,"length":0.8},"text":{"formula":"…and tied at both ends, only whole waves fit"}}}]},"hook":{"text":"What is a wave, really?"},"youtube":{"title":"What is a wave? Explained in 30 seconds 🌊 #shorts","description":"Step 1: in a wave nothing travels but the shape; each bead only moves up and down. Step 2: speed = frequency × wavelength, so a shorter wave is a higher note. Step 3: a string tied at both ends only fits whole half-waves: the harmonics.\n\n#satisfying #simulation #shorts","tags":"math, physics, waves, sound, education, learn, science, satisfying, shorts"}},{"name":"Lesson · The Harmonic Series","mode":"circle","transport":{"duration":33,"bpm":100},"visual":{"aspect":"9:16"},"overlay":{"layout":4,"name":{"enabled":false}},"audio":{"scale":"minorPentatonic","root":9,"noteLow":50,"noteHigh":86,"reverb":{"decay":3.5,"mix":0.35},"delay":{"time":0.375,"feedback":0.3,"mix":0.16}},"circle":{"rule":"string","lab":{"string":{"modes":"1 2 3 4 5 6","every":3}},"look":{"trail":"lines","palette":"aurora","ambience":true},"sound":{"instrument":"harp","pitch":"angle","volume":0.7,"maxRate":22,"length":0.8},"text":{"formula":"A string: 1, 2, 3 … loops…"},"combine":"sequence","layers":[{"id":"C2","enabled":true,"withPrev":false,"cfg":{"rule":"tubes","lab":{"tubes":{"pipe":"open","count":6,"every":1.5,"wait":0.3}},"look":{"trail":"lines","palette":"gold"},"sound":{"instrument":"marimba","pitch":"angle","volume":0.7,"maxRate":22,"length":0.6},"text":{"formula":"…an open tube: the same notes…"}}},{"id":"C3","enabled":true,"withPrev":false,"cfg":{"rule":"tubes","lab":{"tubes":{"pipe":"half","count":6,"every":1.5,"wait":0.3}},"look":{"trail":"lines","palette":"gold"},"sound":{"instrument":"marimba","pitch":"angle","volume":0.7,"maxRate":22,"length":0.6},"text":{"formula":"…close one end: only the odd ones"}}}]},"hook":{"text":"Why do instruments sound different?"},"youtube":{"title":"The harmonic series: why a flute and a clarinet differ 🎶 #shorts","description":"A string and an open tube can sing every harmonic: 1, 2, 3, 4 … A tube closed at one end can only sing the odd ones: 1, 3, 5 … The dust in the tubes shows where the air stands still.\n\n#satisfying #simulation #shorts","tags":"math, physics, waves, sound, education, learn, science, satisfying, shorts"}},{"name":"Lesson · Any Wave from Circles","mode":"circle","transport":{"duration":33,"bpm":100},"visual":{"aspect":"9:16"},"overlay":{"layout":4,"name":{"enabled":false}},"audio":{"scale":"minorPentatonic","root":9,"noteLow":50,"noteHigh":86,"reverb":{"decay":3.5,"mix":0.35},"delay":{"time":0.375,"feedback":0.3,"mix":0.16}},"circle":{"rule":"fourier","